m running near even in his home state, the race could move toward the tossup range.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Kentucky remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, and the fundamentals point firmly that way. The state's partisan lean sits around fifteen points to the right of the country, and recent presidential contests there have been decided by wide margins. With no candidate polling, no expert ratings, and no market pricing available, the forecast rests almost entirely on those fundamentals.\n\nThe main wrinkle is Andy Beshear, a twice-elected Democratic governor whose national profile and rising popularity have drawn attention as a possible presidential contender. Headlines also point to a Democratic surge that could reach Republican-leaning states. Even so, no nominee has emerged, and a governor's home-state popularity does not automatically transfer to a presidential ticket. The forecast expects the Republican nominee to carry Kentucky by roughly fourteen points, with the caveat that a Beshear-led ticket could narrow the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0468, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1024, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.09, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1001, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1139, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.083, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1075, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0972, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0925, "margin": -14.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.1205, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1537, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-